---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import AspectRatioCalculator from '@/components/tools/units/AspectRatioCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="screen-size-calculator">
  <AspectRatioCalculator client:load mode="screen" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The diagonal is the least useful screen size</h2>
    <p>
      Screens are sold by diagonal because it is one large-sounding number. You seldom need it: what
      matters is whether the panel fits the desk, the wall recess or the AV cabinet, and how tall it is on
      a stand. Two screens with the same diagonal can differ by ten centimetres in width and by a third in
      area, because their shapes differ. The screen size calculator gives the width, height and area.
    </p>

    <h2>The screen size formula</h2>
    <p>
      A screen is a rectangle, so the diagonal is its hypotenuse. With <code>r = width ÷ height</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>width  = diagonal × r ÷ √(r² + 1)
height = diagonal ÷ √(r² + 1)
area   = width × height</code></pre>
    <p>
      For a 27-inch 16:9 panel, r = 1.7778 and √(r² + 1) = 2.0396, which gives 23.53 in wide and 13.24 in
      tall (59.8 × 33.6 cm). The calculator shows those two lines with your own numbers, so you can check
      the result.
    </p>

    <h2>Common screen sizes</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Diagonal</th><th>Ratio</th><th>Width</th><th>Height</th><th>Area</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>24 in</td><td>16:9</td><td>20.9 in / 53.1 cm</td><td>11.8 in / 29.9 cm</td><td>246 in²</td></tr>
          <tr><td>27 in</td><td>16:9</td><td>23.5 in / 59.8 cm</td><td>13.2 in / 33.6 cm</td><td>311 in²</td></tr>
          <tr><td>32 in</td><td>16:9</td><td>27.9 in / 70.8 cm</td><td>15.7 in / 39.8 cm</td><td>437 in²</td></tr>
          <tr><td>34 in</td><td>64:27</td><td>31.4 in / 79.7 cm</td><td>13.2 in / 33.6 cm</td><td>414 in²</td></tr>
          <tr><td>55 in</td><td>16:9</td><td>47.9 in / 121.7 cm</td><td>27.0 in / 68.6 cm</td><td>1294 in²</td></tr>
          <tr><td>65 in</td><td>16:9</td><td>56.7 in / 143.9 cm</td><td>31.9 in / 81.0 cm</td><td>1806 in²</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The 34-inch ultrawide is wider than a 32-inch 16:9 monitor, but it is shorter and has a smaller total
      area. A bigger diagonal does not always mean more screen.
    </p>

    <h2>Pixel density, and what counts as sharp</h2>
    <p>
      PPI is the diagonal in pixels divided by the diagonal in inches: <code>PPI = √(w² + h²) ÷ diagonal</code>.
      A 27-inch 1440p monitor is 109 PPI; the same size at 4K is 163; a 14-inch laptop at 2880 × 1800 is
      about 243.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>PPI</th><th>Impression at desk distance</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Under 90</td><td>Visibly pixelated; text edges look stepped</td></tr>
          <tr><td>90–110</td><td>The traditional desktop norm; fully usable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>110–140</td><td>Noticeably cleaner text</td></tr>
          <tr><td>140–200</td><td>Sharp; most people stop noticing pixels</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Over 200</td><td>Retina-class: individual pixels are invisible at normal distance</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Sharpness depends on distance as much as density. A 65-inch 4K television is only 68 PPI, but at three
      metres it looks sharp. The eye resolves about one arcminute, so pixels per degree of vision matter, not
      pixels per inch of glass.
    </p>

    <h2>Buying and fitting</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Add the bezel and stand. The calculated figures are the active display area. Real products are 1–3 cm wider and much taller with the stand.</li>
      <li>Check the height for wall mounts. A 65-inch television is 81 cm tall, which in an alcove is often the limit, more than the width.</li>
      <li>Two 24-inch monitors ≠ one 32-inch. Two 24s give 42 in of width and 492 in² of area; a 32 gives 28 in and 437 in². The pair is larger on both counts but has a bezel down the middle.</li>
      <li>Ultrawide versus dual: a 34-inch 21:9 is close to two 19-inch panels side by side, without the seam, but you cannot angle the halves.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Phones and tablets</h2>
    <p>
      The same maths applies. Enter the diagonal, the ratio (19.5:9 and 20:9 for modern phones, 4:3 or 3:2
      for tablets) and the resolution. Manufacturers measure the diagonal as if the display were a plain
      rectangle, so a phone with a notch or rounded corners has slightly less usable area than the number
      suggests.
    </p>

    <h2>Related tools</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/aspect-ratio-calculator">Aspect Ratio Calculator</a> for pixel dimensions,
      <a href="/cm-to-inches">CM to Inches</a> for the unit conversion, and
      <a href="/px-to-rem-converter">PX to REM Converter</a> for the CSS side of screen sizing.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
